  "query_preview": "..A weak shortwave crossing the region embedded in\nquasi-zonal flow continues to shift across northern Nevada into \nnorthern Utah through the overnight hours. Mainly high-based \nshowers are noted on radar moving into far western Utah. A few \nlocations may see a period of light rain overnight, but widespread\nrain is not expected. Given decoupling, don't expect much of a \ngusty, microburst wind threat with this high-based convection.\n\nAs broad troughing engulfs the Pacific Northwest, the upper level\nridge will continue to flatten, allowing for modest moisture\nadvection to shift into southern Utah Friday. The bulk of the CAMS\nindicate that the highest threat of isolated to widely scattered\nthunderstorms will be across southern and eastern Utah Friday\nafternoon into Friday evening. Given the net moisture increase,\nWPC has outlooked this area in a marginal risk for excessive\nrainfall. This correlates well with conceptual models and\nguidance. The flash flood potential product for the southern Utah\nnational park and rec areas has every location in a \"possible\" for\nFriday. \n\nBy Saturday, weak subsidence will likely suppress convection in\nmost locations, though isolated thunderstorms across far eastern\nportions of the CWA including the Swell and Capitol Reef area will\nrepresent a low end flash flood threat.  \n\nAs early as Saturday evening, another period of moisture advection\nwill shift into southern Utah ahead of a strong cold front. This\nfront continues to slow down in each iteration of guidance...now\nlooking to reach northwestern Utah after sunrise Sunday, reaching\nsouthern Utah by Monday morning. This may provide another window\nwith enhanced moisture for another round of at least isolated to\nwidely scattered thunderstorms across southern and eastern Utah\nahead of the cold front.\n\nAs far as gusty winds ahead of the cold front, it will be a battle\nbetween decoupling and a strong gradient whether northern valleys\nwill see advisory level winds Saturday evening/night into early \nMonday. NBM probs still suggest around a 50% chance wind advisory \ncriteria will be met, but the later this cold front shifts, the \nless likely wind advisory criteria will be met.\n\nMuch cooler temperatures will build into the region by Monday.\nGiven the slower cold front timing, the cooldown Sunday isn't as\naggressive, around 10-15 degrees across northern Utah, with around\nanother 5 degrees of cooling by Monday.\n\nLonger range, southern and eastern Utah is outlooked in a \nmoderate risk for heavy precipitation between the 18th and 20th.\nSomething to keep an eye on...and an indication that an extended\nperiod of dry conditions is the less likely outcome through the\nnext few weeks.",
